/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A power diode has three layers: a heavily doped p+ anode (A), a lightly doped n− drift region and a heavily doped n+ cathode (K).
   - Forward bias: almost no current flows until vD passes the threshold (cut-in) voltage VTO, about 0.7 V to 0.8 V for silicon.
   - vD = VTO + iD rd
   - where vD is the on-state voltage (V), VTO the threshold voltage (V), iD the forward current (A) and rd the slope resistance (Ω).
   - Reverse bias: only a small leakage current IR flows until the breakdown voltage VBR.
   - Turn-on: for a short forward recovery time tfr the drift region is not yet flooded with carriers, so the voltage overshoots its steady value.
   - Turn-off: the circuit forces the current down at a rate di/dt set by the supply voltage and circuit inductance.
   - Qrr ≈ ½ IRRM trr
   - the triangle approximation, with Qrr in coulombs, IRRM in amperes and trr in seconds.
   - The softness factor is S = tb / ta.
*/
