/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The gate-cathode path is a p-n junction, so its V–I curve is like a diode's and it varies from one device to the next.
   - Rs = (Vs − VGT) / IGT
   - where Rs is the series gate resistor (Ω), Vs the trigger source voltage (V), VGT the gate trigger voltage (V) and IGT the gate trigger current (A).
   - A good gate pulse rises in well under a microsecond with an initial current several times IGT (hard triggering), so the whole cathode turns on quickly.
   - R must lie between two limits for the UJT to oscillate: below (VBB − VP)/IP so that it can supply the peak-point current IP, and above (VBB − VV)/IV so that the UJT can turn off at the valley point (VV, IV).
*/
