/* book Worked example 2: Q: A UJT trigger circuit has VBB = 20 V, η = 0.63, VD = 0.7 V, R = 10 kΩ and C = 0.1 µF. Find the peak-point voltage and the frequency of the pulses. The UJT has IP = 10 µA, VV = 2 V and IV = 4 mA; check that R is in the working range.
   1. VP = η VBB + VD = 0.63 × 20 + 0.7 = 13.3 V.
   2. Time constant: R C = 10 × 103 × 0.1 × 10−6 = 1 ms.
   3. T = R C ln(1/(1 − η)) = 1 ms × ln(1 ÷ 0.37) = 1 ms × 0.994 = 0.994 ms, so f = 1 ÷ 0.994 ms = 1.006 kHz ≈ 1 kHz.
   4. Check with the valley voltage (the capacitor restarts from VV, not 0): T = R C ln((VBB − VV)/(VBB − VP)) = 1 ms × ln(18 ÷ 6.7) = 0.988 ms, within 1 % of step 3.
   5. Limits: Rmax = (VBB − VP)/IP = 6.7 V ÷ 10 µA = 670 kΩ; Rmin = (VBB − VV)/IV = 18 V ÷ 4 mA = 4.5 kΩ. 10 kΩ lies between them.
   Answer: VP = 13.3 V and the pulses come at about 1 kHz; R = 10 kΩ is inside the 4.5 kΩ to 670 kΩ range. */
